    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">
      <section class="mb-12">
        <p class="font-body text-on-surface leading-relaxed mb-6">Big-name e-commerce keeps the spotlight, but Malaysia's retail backbone is still the kedai runcit, sundry shop, and mini-market — the corner shop in Bandar Tasik Selatan, the family-owned grocery in Kampung Pandan, the Indian sundry store in Brickfields. Every one of them buys cardboard boxes, tape, and bubble wrap weekly. Most of them buy from the same overpriced wholesalers their grandparents did, because nobody's offered them a better deal. That's the opening for a reseller. Quinocycle supplies the wholesale stock; you build the route.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="mb-12">
        <h2 class="font-headline text-2xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-6">Why Kedai Runcit Need Packaging</h2>
        <h3 class="font-headline text-xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-4">Stock Receiving</h3>
        <p class="font-body text-on-surface leading-relaxed mb-6">Distributors deliver to sundry shops in cartons. Those cartons get reused by the shop owner — for storage in the back room, for repacking bulk into smaller customer portions, or for sending stuff to the family kampung when a relative passes through. There's a steady churn through the inventory.</p>

        <h3 class="font-headline text-xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-4">Repacking Bulk into Retail Portions</h3>
        <p class="font-body text-on-surface leading-relaxed mb-6">Sugar, rice, flour, dried chillies, sambal ikan bilis — sundry shops buy 25kg sacks and sell to customers in 1kg, 2kg, or 5kg portions. Small carton inserts and PE bags are part of this workflow. Resellers who supply both win on convenience.</p>

        <h3 class="font-headline text-xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-4">Courier & Pos Laju Dispatch</h3>
        <p class="font-body text-on-surface leading-relaxed mb-6">Many sundry shops now act as informal courier consolidation points — accepting parcels from Shopee/Lazada returns, mailing items for elderly customers, sending kueh to relatives. They need a small stock of clean cartons in standard sizes plus tape and bubble wrap.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="mb-12">
        <h2 class="font-headline text-2xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-6">Building a Route as a Reseller</h2>
        <h3 class="font-headline text-xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-4">Identifying Your Territory</h3>
        <p class="font-body text-on-surface leading-relaxed mb-6">Pick a 5-10km radius you can cover from your home or vehicle base. Start by listing the kedai runcit you already see daily — your neighbourhood, near your workplace, on your school-run route. A typical reseller starts with 30-50 shops and grows from there.</p>

        <h3 class="font-headline text-xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-4">Pricing & Margin</h3>
        <p class="font-body text-on-surface leading-relaxed mb-6">Resellers typically mark up wholesale carton prices 25-40%. The shop owner cares about three things: convenience (you come to them), reliability (you turn up when promised), and price within reason (not the cheapest, but not gouging). Most shops pay cash on delivery; a few graduate to weekly billing.</p>

        <h3 class="font-headline text-xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-4">Stock Mix to Carry</h3>
        <p class="font-body text-on-surface leading-relaxed mb-6">Carry 4-5 carton sizes — small, medium, large, and one or two specialty (long for fluorescent tubes, square for cake boxes). Plus 2 widths of bubble wrap, transparent and brown packing tape, twine, and PE bags. Avoid overstocking exotic SKUs — the staples turn fastest.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="mb-12">
        <h2 class="font-headline text-2xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-6">Long-Tail Customer Types in Your Area</h2>
        <p class="font-body text-on-surface leading-relaxed mb-6">Beyond pure kedai runcit, the same route serves laundry shops (boxes for stored clothes), photocopy/print shops (paper packaging), tuition centres (book and exercise storage), neighbourhood bakeries (cake boxes), florists (display boxes), pet shops (transport boxes), and hardware stores (parts storage). Build relationships with all of them — your delivery van is already passing the door.</p>
      </section>

